Requested via a coordinator feature-gap review, 2026-08-30. Extension repo:
../lsp-openplanet-ext/ (packages/vscode-ext). Enhancement.

## Gap
The extension's `contributes` has no `snippets` entry at all — verified on
master. Every Openplanet plugin starts from the same handful of shapes and the
user types all of them by hand or copies from another plugin.

## Why this one first among the extension items
It is pure `contributes.snippets` plus a JSON file. No server work, no TypeScript,
no new LSP capability, and it pays off on the very first plugin a user writes.
It is the cheapest real improvement on the whole review list.

## Work
Add a snippets file for the `angelscript` language covering at least:

**Entry points / callbacks** — take the authoritative list from the Openplanet
docs rather than memory, and cover the ones plugins actually use:
- `void Main()`, `void Update(float dt)`, `void Render()`,
  `void RenderMenu()`, `void RenderInterface()`, `void RenderEarly()`,
  `void OnDestroyed()`, `void OnDisabled()`, `void OnEnabled()`,
  `void OnSettingsChanged()`, `void OnKeyPress(bool down, VirtualKey key)`,
  `void OnMouseButton(bool down, int button, int x, int y)`,
  `void OnMouseMove(int x, int y)`, `void OnMouseWheel(int x, int y)`

**Settings blocks** — the `[Setting ...]` attribute forms:
- plain, with `name=`, `description=`, `category=`, `min=`/`max=` for numerics,
  `color` variant, `drag` variant. One snippet per shape, with tab stops on
  the values a user always edits.

**Meta / coroutines**
- `Meta::PluginCoroutine@ ...` / `startnew(...)` shapes
- a `while (true) { yield(); }` coroutine skeleton

**info.toml scaffold** — a snippet for the manifest is also worth having, but
info.toml is TOML not AngelScript, so it needs its own language scoping.
Include it only if it can be scoped without hijacking every TOML file the
user opens; otherwise file it separately rather than degrading TOML editing.

## Design decision (coordinator, 2026-08-30)
Snippet prefixes must be namespaced enough not to fight ordinary typing —
prefer `op` prefixes (`opmain`, `opsetting`, `oprender`) over bare `main` /
`render`, which would insert on any identifier starting that way. A snippet
that fires when the user meant to type a variable name is a net negative.

Verify the callback SIGNATURES against the Openplanet docs before writing
them. A snippet with a wrong parameter list is worse than no snippet: it
compiles, silently never fires, and the user has no reason to doubt it. Cite
the docs URL in a comment in the snippets file.

## Test plan
Snippets are declarative, so the meaningful tests are structural (vitest, the
extension repo's existing harness):
- the snippets file is valid JSON and every entry has `prefix`, `body`,
  `description`
- every `prefix` is unique across the file
- every prefix starts with the agreed namespace
- tab stops are well-formed and sequential (`$1`, `$2`, ..., `$0` present and
  last) — a malformed tab stop breaks the insert silently
- `contributes.snippets` in package.json points at a file that exists and
  declares `"language": "angelscript"`
- placeholder text contains no smart quotes or non-ASCII that would break in
  a source file

Manual gate before closing: insert every snippet into a scratch plugin and
confirm the result compiles under `openplanet-lsp check` with no new
diagnostics. A snippet that emits code our own checker flags is a bug.

